  AcademyCourse(
    title: 'Economics',
    marks: '200 marks • Group I',
    description: 'A structured foundation in microeconomics, macroeconomics, money and banking, public finance, international trade and Pakistan’s economy.',
    chapters: [
      AcademyChapter(number: '01', title: 'Introduction to economics and microeconomic foundations', syllabusLink: 'Scarcity, choice, demand, supply, elasticity, consumer behaviour and production', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Scarcity, opportunity cost, demand and supply', objectives: 'Use core economic concepts to explain how individuals and markets allocate scarce resources.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Scarcity and choice', '''Economics studies how individuals and societies allocate scarce resources among competing uses. Scarcity does not mean that a resource is rare in an absolute sense; it means available resources are limited relative to wants. Every choice therefore has an opportunity cost: the value of the next-best alternative forgone. A production possibility frontier illustrates the combinations of two goods an economy can produce with given resources and technology. Points on the frontier are productively efficient; points inside indicate underutilisation; points beyond are currently unattainable.

Economic reasoning separates positive statements, which can be tested against evidence, from normative statements, which involve value judgments. CSS answers should identify assumptions, define the relevant market or time period and distinguish a causal claim from a correlation.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Demand, supply and equilibrium', '''The law of demand states that, other things equal, quantity demanded generally falls as price rises. A demand curve shifts when non-price determinants change, such as income, preferences, prices of substitutes and complements, expectations or the number of buyers. The law of supply states that quantity supplied generally rises with price, other things equal. Supply shifts with input costs, technology, taxes, subsidies, expectations and the number of sellers.

Market equilibrium occurs where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied. A price ceiling below equilibrium can create excess demand; a price floor above equilibrium can create excess supply. These outcomes depend on enforcement, market structure and adjustment over time. Do not confuse a movement along a curve, caused by a change in the good’s own price, with a shift of the entire curve.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Elasticity and policy application', '''Price elasticity of demand measures the percentage change in quantity demanded divided by the percentage change in price. Demand may be elastic, inelastic or unit elastic. Elasticity depends on substitutes, necessity, budget share and time to adjust. It helps explain why a tax may raise substantial revenue in one market but sharply reduce consumption in another. Cross-price elasticity measures responsiveness to another good’s price; income elasticity measures responsiveness to income.

For an applied answer, explain who bears a tax using relative elasticities rather than assuming that the legally responsible party bears the economic burden. Draw a labelled diagram where useful and state the short-run or long-run context.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Scarcity', 'Opportunity cost', 'Production possibility frontier', 'Demand', 'Supply', 'Equilibrium', 'Price elasticity', 'Tax incidence'], examQuestion: 'Explain how demand and supply determine market equilibrium. Discuss the effects of a binding price ceiling.', practiceTask: 'Draw demand-supply diagrams for a price ceiling, an indirect tax and a subsidy. Explain the effect on consumers, producers and government revenue.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '02', title: 'Consumer behaviour, production and market structures', syllabusLink: 'Utility, indifference curves, costs, revenue, perfect competition, monopoly and imperfect competition', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Consumer choice, costs and market power', objectives: 'Explain how consumers and firms make decisions and compare major market structures.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Utility and consumer equilibrium', '''Utility is a framework for analysing preferences. The cardinal approach assigns numerical utility, while the ordinal approach ranks bundles without requiring measurable satisfaction. Indifference curves show combinations of goods that provide equal satisfaction; under standard assumptions they slope downward and are convex to the origin. The budget line represents affordable combinations. Consumer equilibrium occurs where the highest attainable indifference curve is tangent to the budget constraint, subject to the model’s assumptions.

A change in income shifts the budget line; a change in relative prices rotates it. The substitution effect captures a response to changed relative prices, while the income effect captures the change in purchasing power. Use these concepts to analyse the likely effects of inflation or a targeted subsidy.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Production and costs', '''Production converts inputs into outputs. In the short run at least one factor is fixed; in the long run all factors can be varied. The law of diminishing marginal returns describes how, with other inputs fixed, adding more of a variable input eventually produces smaller increments of output. Costs include fixed, variable, average and marginal costs. Marginal cost is the additional cost of producing one more unit and is central to the firm’s output decision.

Economies of scale occur when long-run average cost falls as output expands; diseconomies arise when coordination and management costs eventually increase. Distinguish internal economies, arising within a firm, from external economies, arising from industry-wide conditions.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Competition and monopoly', '''Under perfect competition, many firms sell a homogeneous product and individual firms are price takers under the model’s assumptions. A monopolist faces the market demand curve and may restrict output relative to a competitive benchmark, creating a potential deadweight loss. Monopolistic competition combines product differentiation with entry by many firms. Oligopoly involves strategic interdependence: each firm’s decision depends on expected responses by rivals.

Market structure affects prices, innovation, consumer choice and efficiency. Real markets rarely match textbook assumptions perfectly. Evaluate concentration alongside barriers to entry, contestability, regulation and the possibility of innovation.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Utility', 'Indifference curve', 'Budget constraint', 'Marginal product', 'Marginal cost', 'Economies of scale', 'Monopoly', 'Oligopoly'], examQuestion: 'Compare perfect competition and monopoly with reference to output, price and allocative efficiency.', practiceTask: 'Prepare a comparison table for four market structures and identify one plausible Pakistani market example for each, noting limitations of the comparison.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '03', title: 'Macroeconomics, national income and stabilisation', syllabusLink: 'National income, aggregate demand and supply, inflation, unemployment, growth and fiscal and monetary policy', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'GDP, inflation, unemployment and macroeconomic policy', objectives: 'Interpret macroeconomic indicators and explain how fiscal and monetary tools influence economic activity.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. National income accounting', '''Gross domestic product measures the market value of final goods and services produced within a country during a period. It can be estimated through production, expenditure or income approaches. The expenditure identity is GDP = C + I + G + (X − M), where consumption, investment, government purchases and net exports are measured consistently. Nominal GDP uses current prices; real GDP adjusts for price changes. GDP per capita is an average and does not reveal income distribution, unpaid work or environmental costs.

Distinguish GDP from gross national income, which accounts for primary income flows with the rest of the world. A sound answer explains the measure’s usefulness and limitations rather than treating GDP growth as a complete measure of welfare.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Inflation and unemployment', '''Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level, commonly measured through a consumer price index. Demand-pull inflation arises when aggregate demand grows faster than productive capacity; cost-push inflation follows increases in production costs or supply disruptions. Expectations can make inflation persistent. Disinflation means a slower rate of price increase; deflation means a fall in the general price level.

Unemployment measures people without work who are available and actively seeking employment under the relevant statistical definition. Frictional, structural and cyclical unemployment have different causes and policy responses. Labour-force participation and underemployment matter when interpreting headline rates. Explain the distributional effects of inflation, especially on households with fixed or low incomes.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Fiscal and monetary stabilisation', '''Fiscal policy changes taxation, public spending and borrowing. Expansionary fiscal policy can support demand during a downturn, while contractionary measures may reduce demand pressures. Monetary policy influences financial conditions through policy rates, liquidity operations and communication. Central-bank independence, credibility and transmission mechanisms affect its effectiveness.

Policy choices involve trade-offs. Tight monetary policy may reduce inflation but also slow investment and employment; expansionary fiscal policy may support growth but create debt and external-balance pressures if poorly designed. Evaluate timing, targeting, implementation capacity and the state of the business cycle.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['GDP', 'Real GDP', 'National income', 'CPI', 'Demand-pull inflation', 'Cost-push inflation', 'Fiscal policy', 'Monetary policy'], examQuestion: 'Discuss the causes of inflation and critically evaluate the effectiveness of monetary policy in controlling it.', practiceTask: 'Write a macroeconomic diagnosis using GDP growth, inflation, unemployment, fiscal balance and current-account indicators. Separate symptoms from structural causes.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '04', title: 'Money, banking, international trade and Pakistan’s economy', syllabusLink: 'Money and banking, exchange rates, balance of payments, trade theory, development and Pakistan’s economic structure', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Banking, trade and economic development', objectives: 'Explain financial intermediation, exchange-rate adjustment, trade gains and structural development challenges.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Money and banking', '''Money performs the functions of medium of exchange, unit of account and store of value. Commercial banks accept deposits and extend credit, while central banks conduct monetary policy, support payment systems and contribute to financial stability. Credit creation is constrained by capital, liquidity, regulation, risk appetite and demand for borrowing; it is not an unlimited mechanical process.

Financial inclusion concerns access to useful and affordable financial services. A strong answer links inclusion to identity systems, consumer protection, digital infrastructure, competition and financial literacy. Banking stability requires supervision, sound risk management and credible resolution arrangements.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. International trade and balance of payments', '''Comparative advantage explains how countries can gain from specialisation when relative opportunity costs differ, even if one country is more productive in every good. Trade policy includes tariffs, quotas, subsidies, standards and trade agreements. These instruments redistribute gains and losses across consumers, producers and government, and may invite retaliation or protect inefficient activity if poorly designed.

The balance of payments records transactions between residents and non-residents. The current account includes goods, services, primary income and secondary income; the financial account records cross-border investment and financial flows under the applicable accounting framework. Exchange-rate movements affect import costs, export competitiveness and foreign-currency debt, but the outcome depends on pass-through, contracts and supply responsiveness.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Pakistan’s development challenges', '''Pakistan’s economy must be analysed through productivity, human capital, energy, taxation, exports, investment, agriculture, industry, services and institutional quality. Persistent constraints can include a narrow tax base, low export diversification, external financing needs, energy-sector inefficiencies, weak learning outcomes and climate vulnerability. These are interconnected rather than isolated problems.

A serious reform agenda prioritises sequencing and political feasibility. Broadening the tax base requires fair administration and reduced exemptions; export growth requires reliable energy, logistics, skills, standards and predictable policy; human-capital investment needs measurable improvements in learning and health. Avoid unsupported single-cause explanations and use dated official data when citing current indicators.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Central bank', 'Financial intermediation', 'Comparative advantage', 'Tariff', 'Balance of payments', 'Current account', 'Exchange rate', 'Productivity'], examQuestion: 'Identify the structural constraints on Pakistan’s economic development and propose a prioritised reform strategy.', practiceTask: 'Prepare a two-page policy brief: diagnose three structural constraints, propose sequenced reforms, identify implementation risks and define measurable indicators.'),
      ]),
    ],
  ),
  AcademyCourse(
    title: 'Computer Science',
    marks: '200 marks • Group I',
    description: 'A concept-first course covering computer fundamentals, programming, data structures, databases, operating systems, networks, software engineering, security and AI foundations.',
    chapters: [
      AcademyChapter(number: '01', title: 'Computer systems and data representation', syllabusLink: 'Computer architecture, hardware, software, number systems, data representation and logic', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'How a computer represents and processes information', objectives: 'Explain the stored-program model, major hardware components and binary representation.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. The stored-program computer', '''A computer is an electronic system that accepts input, processes data according to instructions, stores information and produces output. In the stored-program model, instructions and data are represented in memory. The central processing unit fetches, decodes and executes instructions, coordinating arithmetic/logic operations and movement of data. The control unit directs operations; the arithmetic logic unit performs arithmetic and logical comparisons; registers provide very fast storage close to the processor.

The memory hierarchy balances speed, capacity and cost. Registers and cache are small and fast; main memory is larger but volatile; secondary storage provides persistent capacity. The distinction between volatile and non-volatile storage is essential when explaining what survives a power loss.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Binary numbers and data encoding', '''Digital systems represent information using bits, each with two possible states. A byte commonly consists of eight bits. Binary place values are powers of two; hexadecimal provides a compact representation because one hexadecimal digit corresponds to four bits. Signed integers can be represented using two’s complement, while floating-point formats represent a sign, exponent and significand with finite precision.

Text, images, audio and video are encoded into digital representations. Character encodings such as Unicode support multilingual text. Image representation uses pixels and colour values; audio sampling measures a signal at discrete intervals and quantises amplitude. Resolution, sampling rate, bit depth and compression influence quality and storage requirements.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Logic and performance', '''Boolean algebra operates on true/false values and underpins digital logic. AND, OR and NOT gates can be combined into circuits; NAND and NOR are functionally complete gates. Truth tables show outputs for all input combinations. Performance depends on more than clock frequency: instruction throughput, core count, memory latency, cache behaviour, parallelism and workload characteristics all matter.

Amdahl’s law explains why the serial portion of a program limits the benefit of parallel processing. In an exam answer, distinguish theoretical peak performance from measured performance on a defined workload.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['CPU', 'ALU', 'Control unit', 'Register', 'Cache', 'Volatile memory', 'Two’s complement', 'Boolean algebra'], examQuestion: 'Explain the fetch-decode-execute cycle and discuss the role of the memory hierarchy in computer performance.', practiceTask: 'Convert selected decimal values to binary and hexadecimal; construct truth tables for AND, OR, XOR and NOT; explain one performance bottleneck.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '02', title: 'Programming fundamentals and problem solving', syllabusLink: 'Algorithms, flowcharts, programming paradigms, data types, control structures, functions and debugging', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Algorithms, control flow and modular programming', objectives: 'Translate a problem into a clear algorithm and explain core programming constructs.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Algorithms and complexity', '''An algorithm is a finite, unambiguous sequence of steps for solving a problem. A good algorithm has defined inputs and outputs, correctness, finiteness and effective operations. Flowcharts and pseudocode help communicate logic before implementation. Problem solving begins by clarifying requirements, constraints, edge cases and the expected form of output.

Time complexity describes how running time grows with input size; space complexity describes additional memory use. Big-O notation expresses an asymptotic upper bound, often used to compare growth rates. Constant, logarithmic, linear, linearithmic, quadratic and exponential growth behave very differently as input size increases. State the assumptions and identify the dominant operation when analysing complexity.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Variables, types and control structures', '''Variables name values stored during program execution. Data types define the kind of values and operations supported, such as integers, floating-point numbers, characters, strings and Boolean values. Expressions combine values and operators; assignment changes a variable’s stored value. Selection uses conditions to choose a path, commonly through if/else structures. Iteration repeats instructions using loops.

Loop correctness requires an initial condition, a termination condition and progress toward termination. Common errors include off-by-one boundaries, incorrect Boolean logic, unintended type conversion and infinite loops. Explain a loop using a trace table that records variable values at each iteration.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Functions, abstraction and testing', '''Functions package reusable operations behind defined interfaces. Parameters provide inputs; return values communicate results. Local scope limits where a variable is accessible and helps prevent unintended interactions. Modular design separates a large problem into smaller units that can be understood, tested and maintained independently.

Testing checks behaviour against expected results. Unit tests focus on small components; integration tests examine interactions; system tests evaluate the complete application. Boundary-value and equivalence-partition testing help select useful test cases. Debugging identifies and corrects causes rather than merely hiding symptoms.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Algorithm', 'Pseudocode', 'Big-O', 'Variable', 'Data type', 'Iteration', 'Function', 'Unit testing'], examQuestion: 'Define an algorithm and explain how time complexity helps compare alternative solutions.', practiceTask: 'Design an algorithm to find the largest value in a list, write pseudocode, trace it on sample input and state its time complexity.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '03', title: 'Data structures, databases and operating systems', syllabusLink: 'Arrays, linked lists, stacks, queues, trees, searching, sorting, DBMS concepts, OS processes and memory', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Organising data and managing computer resources', objectives: 'Compare common data structures and explain database and operating-system responsibilities.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Data structures and algorithms', '''An array stores elements in indexed positions and supports efficient direct access when the index is known. A linked list stores elements in nodes connected by references; insertion and deletion can be efficient when the relevant node is known, but access generally requires traversal. A stack follows last-in, first-out order; a queue follows first-in, first-out order. Trees represent hierarchical relationships, while graphs represent more general networks.

Searching and sorting algorithms have different time and space costs. Linear search examines elements sequentially; binary search requires sorted data and repeatedly halves the search interval. Merge sort has O(n log n) time complexity in typical analysis and uses additional memory; simple quadratic sorts may be suitable only for small or nearly ordered data. Explain preconditions before claiming an algorithm is applicable.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Database management systems', '''A database is an organised collection of related data; a database management system provides facilities to define, query, update and protect it. Relational databases organise data into tables connected through keys. A primary key identifies a row; a foreign key represents a relationship and supports referential integrity. SQL is used to define and manipulate relational data.

Normalisation reduces undesirable redundancy and update anomalies. First normal form requires atomic values under the usual relational model; second and third normal forms address partial and transitive dependencies. Transactions are commonly described through ACID properties: atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability. Indexes can accelerate retrieval but add storage and write-maintenance costs.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Operating systems', '''An operating system manages processes, memory, files, devices and security while providing interfaces for applications and users. A process is a program in execution; threads are execution paths within a process that may share resources. Scheduling allocates CPU time among runnable tasks. Concurrency creates coordination challenges such as race conditions, deadlocks and starvation.

Virtual memory gives processes an address-space abstraction and allows the system to manage physical memory through paging or related techniques. File systems organise persistent data and metadata. Explain how protection and access control prevent one process or user from improperly interfering with another.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Array', 'Linked list', 'Stack', 'Queue', 'Binary search', 'Primary key', 'Normalisation', 'Process', 'Deadlock'], examQuestion: 'Compare arrays and linked lists. Explain database normalisation and the ACID properties of transactions.', practiceTask: 'Design a small student-record relational schema with primary and foreign keys; identify one normalisation issue and propose a correction.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '04', title: 'Networks, cybersecurity, software engineering and emerging technologies', syllabusLink: 'Computer networks, Internet protocols, information security, software development, AI and contemporary computing', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Connecting, securing and engineering modern systems', objectives: 'Explain network communication, core security principles and disciplined software development.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Computer networks and the Internet', '''A computer network connects devices to exchange data and share resources. LANs cover limited areas; WANs connect geographically separated networks. Packet switching divides data into packets that are routed independently through networks. The TCP/IP suite provides layered functions: IP addresses and routes packets, while TCP provides reliable ordered delivery and UDP offers lower-overhead datagram transport without the same delivery guarantees.

DNS translates domain names into records used by network clients. HTTP and HTTPS support web communication, with TLS providing encryption and authentication for secure connections. Bandwidth, latency, jitter and packet loss describe different aspects of network performance. Do not use bandwidth and speed as interchangeable explanations without defining the measure.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Cybersecurity foundations', '''Information security is commonly organised around confidentiality, integrity and availability. Threats include phishing, malware, credential theft, insecure configuration, software vulnerabilities and denial-of-service attacks. Risk depends on the likelihood of an event and its potential impact. Controls include strong authentication, least privilege, encryption, secure backups, patch management, monitoring and user awareness.

Encryption protects data using cryptographic algorithms and keys. Hash functions produce fixed-length digests and are useful for integrity checks and password-storage schemes when combined with appropriate password hashing techniques. Digital signatures support authenticity and integrity. A security programme requires governance, incident response, recovery planning and regular testing—not merely installing antivirus software.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Software engineering and AI foundations', '''Software engineering applies disciplined methods to requirements, architecture, implementation, testing, deployment and maintenance. Development approaches include sequential lifecycle models and iterative methods such as Agile. Requirements should be testable and prioritised; architecture should address reliability, security, maintainability and scale. Version control records changes and supports collaboration.

Artificial intelligence concerns systems performing tasks associated with perception, reasoning, language or decision-making. Machine learning learns patterns from data; supervised learning uses labelled examples, unsupervised learning seeks structure in unlabelled data and reinforcement learning learns through interaction and feedback. Models can overfit, inherit bias or fail under distribution shift. Evaluate them with appropriate metrics, representative data, human oversight and clear limits.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['TCP/IP', 'DNS', 'HTTPS', 'Confidentiality', 'Integrity', 'Availability', 'Encryption', 'Agile', 'Machine learning'], examQuestion: 'Discuss the CIA triad and explain how layered security controls protect a networked information system.', practiceTask: 'Create a threat model for a university student portal: identify assets, threats, vulnerabilities, controls and residual risks.'),
      ]),
    ],
  ),


  AcademyCourse(
    title: 'Public Administration',
    marks: '100 marks • Group III',
    description: 'A chapter-wise study of administrative theory, organisation, public personnel, budgeting, accountability, policy implementation and administrative reform.',
    chapters: [
      AcademyChapter(number: '01', title: 'Nature and evolution of public administration', syllabusLink: 'Meaning, scope, public-private administration, politics-administration relationship and major approaches', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Public administration as theory and practice', objectives: 'Define public administration and explain its changing relationship with politics, management and public value.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Meaning, scope and significance', '''Public administration concerns the organisation and management of public institutions and the implementation of laws and public policies. Its scope includes policy advice, service delivery, regulation, public finance, personnel management, intergovernmental coordination and accountability. It is both an academic discipline and a practical activity. Public administrators operate within constitutional rules and political direction while exercising professional judgment in complex implementation settings.

Public administration differs from private management because public agencies must consider legality, equity, transparency, due process and public accountability alongside efficiency. The distinction is not absolute: public organisations may use management techniques from business, while private organisations may perform public functions under regulation or contract.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Politics-administration relationship', '''The classical politics-administration dichotomy sought to distinguish political choices about goals from administrative work implementing them. In practice, administrators contribute to policy through advice, interpretation, implementation design and feedback. Political leaders provide democratic direction, while professional administration supplies continuity, expertise and lawful execution.

The central challenge is not to eliminate interaction but to define responsibility. Administrators should be responsive to elected authority without becoming partisan or acting outside law. Ministers and legislatures must remain accountable for policy choices, while officials are accountable for professional conduct and implementation.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Evolution of approaches', '''Traditional public administration emphasised hierarchy, rules, merit and formal authority. Human-relations approaches highlighted motivation, groups and organisational behaviour. New Public Management promoted performance measurement, managerial discretion, competition and service orientation. New Public Governance stresses networks, collaboration and the interdependence of public, private and civil-society actors. Public value approaches ask whether institutions create outcomes that are legitimate, valuable and operationally feasible.

These approaches are not simple historical replacements. Contemporary administrations often combine rules-based bureaucracy with performance management and collaborative delivery. Evaluate each approach in relation to context, capacity and accountability.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Public administration', 'Public policy', 'Politics-administration dichotomy', 'Weberian bureaucracy', 'New Public Management', 'Governance', 'Public value'], examQuestion: 'Is the politics-administration dichotomy still useful in contemporary public administration? Critically discuss.', practiceTask: 'Analyse a public service delivery problem and separate political choices, administrative responsibilities, legal constraints and citizen feedback.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '02', title: 'Organisation theory and administrative behaviour', syllabusLink: 'Classical, behavioural and modern organisation theories; leadership, motivation, communication and decision-making', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'How public organisations work', objectives: 'Compare organisation theories and apply behavioural concepts to administrative performance.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Classical organisation theory', '''Max Weber’s ideal-type bureaucracy emphasises hierarchy, defined jurisdiction, written rules, specialised offices, merit-based appointment and impersonal administration. These features can promote predictability and equal treatment, particularly in large organisations. However, excessive proceduralism can create rigidity, delay and goal displacement when compliance becomes more important than public outcomes.

Scientific management, associated with Frederick Taylor, sought to improve efficiency through systematic analysis of tasks and work methods. Administrative principles associated with writers such as Henri Fayol focused on management functions and organisational coordination. Treat these as influential frameworks, not universal laws: public organisations face political, legal and ethical constraints that complicate simple efficiency calculations.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Human relations and motivation', '''Human-relations approaches examine informal groups, workplace relationships and the social dimensions of productivity. Motivation theories offer different explanations: Maslow’s hierarchy proposes categories of needs; Herzberg distinguishes hygiene factors from motivators; McGregor contrasts Theory X and Theory Y assumptions about workers. These frameworks are useful for generating questions about incentives and leadership, but should not be applied mechanically to every employee or culture.

Public service motivation explores why people may value meaningful contribution, public interest and service to others. Administrative leaders can support motivation through fair treatment, clear goals, professional development, recognition and opportunities for responsible discretion.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Leadership and decision-making', '''Leadership may be transactional, transformational, participative or situational depending on tasks and organisational conditions. Decision-making in public agencies is constrained by incomplete information, legal requirements, time pressure and competing values. Herbert Simon’s bounded rationality explains why decision-makers satisfice rather than optimise under real-world limits.

Good administrative decisions document evidence, alternatives, risks, affected groups and reasons. Consultation can improve information and legitimacy, but responsibility for the final decision must remain clear. Evaluate leadership by ethical conduct, organisational learning and public outcomes—not charisma alone.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Bureaucracy', 'Hierarchy', 'Scientific management', 'Human relations', 'Motivation', 'Public service motivation', 'Bounded rationality', 'Leadership'], examQuestion: 'Evaluate Weber’s bureaucratic model in light of contemporary demands for responsive and citizen-centred services.', practiceTask: 'Diagnose a fictional department with low morale and slow decisions. Recommend changes in structure, incentives, leadership and accountability.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '03', title: 'Public personnel, budgeting and accountability', syllabusLink: 'Civil service systems, recruitment, training, performance, public budgeting, audit, ethics and accountability', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Managing people, money and integrity', objectives: 'Explain merit-based personnel systems, public budgeting and mechanisms of administrative accountability.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Civil service and human-resource management', '''A professional civil service provides continuity, expertise and impartial implementation. Core functions include workforce planning, recruitment, selection, induction, training, performance appraisal, promotion, discipline and retirement. Merit-based recruitment aims to select candidates through relevant competence and fair procedures. Diversity and inclusion matter because public institutions serve populations with different needs and experiences.

Performance management should connect individual responsibilities to organisational objectives. Poorly designed targets encourage gaming or neglect of unmeasured work. Training is most effective when linked to role requirements, practical application and evaluation of whether performance improves.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Public budgeting and financial control', '''A public budget translates priorities into an authorised financial plan. It estimates revenue and expenditure and provides a basis for legislative scrutiny and executive control. Line-item budgets emphasise inputs; programme budgets connect spending to activities; performance budgets link resources to outputs or outcomes. Medium-term frameworks can improve predictability, but only when forecasts and commitments are credible.

Budget execution requires procurement controls, accounting, internal audit and timely reporting. Value for money considers economy, efficiency and effectiveness. A low-cost purchase is not good value if it fails to deliver the required service or creates higher long-term costs.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Accountability and administrative ethics', '''Accountability means that public officials must explain and justify decisions and face consequences where appropriate. Mechanisms include parliamentary oversight, audit institutions, courts, ombudsman offices, information-access laws, internal controls, media scrutiny and citizen feedback. Each mechanism has different powers and limitations; overlapping institutions can strengthen checks but may also create fragmented responsibility.

Administrative ethics requires legality, impartiality, integrity, avoidance of conflicts of interest, responsible use of public resources and respect for citizens. Codes are insufficient without leadership, safe reporting channels, transparent procedures and credible enforcement. Distinguish individual misconduct from systemic incentives that enable corruption.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Merit system', 'Performance appraisal', 'Programme budgeting', 'Value for money', 'Audit', 'Accountability', 'Conflict of interest', 'Administrative ethics'], examQuestion: 'Discuss how civil-service reform, performance management and accountability can improve public service delivery.', practiceTask: 'Design a simple performance framework for a district health office with three outcomes, measurable indicators, reporting frequency and safeguards against target manipulation.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '04', title: 'Policy implementation, local government and reform in Pakistan', syllabusLink: 'Policy process, implementation, decentralisation, local government, administrative reform and e-governance', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Turning public policy into reliable services', objectives: 'Analyse implementation gaps and propose realistic administrative reforms for Pakistan.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Policy process and implementation', '''The policy process commonly includes problem identification, agenda setting, formulation, adoption, implementation and evaluation. In reality these stages overlap and involve negotiation among elected leaders, ministries, experts, interest groups and citizens. Implementation depends on clear objectives, legal authority, funding, staff, coordination, information systems and local capacity.

A policy may fail even when its design is sound if frontline staff lack resources, responsibilities overlap or incentives reward the wrong behaviour. Implementation analysis therefore asks who must do what, with which resources, under what authority and how progress will be measured.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Decentralisation and local government', '''Decentralisation transfers authority, responsibilities or resources from central government to lower levels. Deconcentration shifts tasks within the same hierarchy; delegation transfers defined responsibilities to semi-autonomous bodies; devolution gives elected subnational governments a stronger autonomous role. Each form has different implications for accountability and local responsiveness.

Local government can improve knowledge of community needs and access to services, but it requires predictable finance, administrative capability, transparent elections and coordination with provincial and federal institutions. Decentralisation without resources or clear responsibility can merely shift blame rather than improve outcomes.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Reform priorities and digital government', '''Administrative reform should begin with a specific service problem and evidence about its causes. Potential measures include simplifying procedures, clarifying decision rights, improving procurement, strengthening frontline staffing, publishing service standards and creating accessible grievance mechanisms. Digital government can reduce transaction costs and improve traceability, but only if systems are secure, interoperable, accessible and supported by non-digital channels for citizens who need them.

For Pakistan, reform proposals should account for federal and provincial responsibilities, fiscal constraints, uneven local capacity and political incentives. Use pilots, independent evaluation and transparent performance reporting before scaling a reform nationally.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Policy cycle', 'Implementation gap', 'Deconcentration', 'Delegation', 'Devolution', 'Local government', 'E-governance', 'Service standards'], examQuestion: 'Why do public policies often suffer implementation gaps in Pakistan? Recommend institutionally feasible remedies.', practiceTask: 'Map one citizen-facing service from application to delivery. Identify delays, responsible offices, legal steps, data requirements and measurable reforms.'),
      ]),
    ],
  ),
  AcademyCourse(
    title: 'Gender Studies',
    marks: '100 marks • Group V',
    description: 'A structured course in gender concepts, feminist theories, social institutions, development, law, political participation and gender policy in Pakistan.',
    chapters: [
      AcademyChapter(number: '01', title: 'Foundations: sex, gender and social construction', syllabusLink: 'Concepts of sex and gender, identity, socialisation, patriarchy and intersectionality', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Understanding gender as a social and analytical category', objectives: 'Distinguish sex and gender and explain how social institutions shape gender roles and inequalities.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Sex, gender and identity', '''Sex commonly refers to biological attributes used in classification, while gender concerns socially organised roles, expectations, identities and relations. The distinction is analytically useful but should not be treated as a claim that biology and society are completely separate in every context. Gender norms vary across time and societies and are reproduced through family, education, media, religion, law, markets and political institutions.

Gender analysis asks how resources, authority, risks and opportunities are distributed. It examines not only women’s experiences but also masculinities, institutional norms and the relationships through which inequality is produced. Use precise definitions and avoid treating any group as internally uniform.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Socialisation and patriarchy', '''Socialisation is the process through which people learn norms and expected behaviours. Families, schools, peers, workplaces, media and public institutions may reinforce or challenge gender expectations. Patriarchy describes social arrangements in which men as a group hold disproportionate authority and control over resources, though its forms vary across class, region, history and institutions.

A careful analysis identifies mechanisms rather than using patriarchy as a complete explanation by itself. Consider inheritance practices, unpaid care work, mobility, occupational segregation, political networks, access to education and enforcement of rights. Distinguish formal legal equality from the lived ability to exercise rights.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Intersectionality', '''Intersectionality examines how gender interacts with class, disability, age, ethnicity, geography and other structures to shape distinct experiences. Kimberlé Crenshaw developed the concept to explain how single-axis frameworks can overlook people situated at intersecting forms of disadvantage. The approach does not mean simply listing identities; it asks how institutions and power relations combine.

For example, a rural low-income woman may face different barriers to transport, schooling or justice than an urban professional woman. Policy analysis should therefore disaggregate evidence and avoid assuming one intervention benefits every group equally.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Sex', 'Gender', 'Gender identity', 'Socialisation', 'Patriarchy', 'Intersectionality', 'Gender norms', 'Unpaid care work'], examQuestion: 'Explain gender as a social construct and discuss the relevance of intersectionality to policy design.', practiceTask: 'Analyse one education or employment barrier using gender, class and geographic factors. Identify evidence needed before proposing a solution.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '02', title: 'Feminist theories and intellectual traditions', syllabusLink: 'Liberal, radical, socialist, Marxist, postcolonial, postmodern and other feminist perspectives', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Comparing feminist approaches', objectives: 'Explain the central claims of major feminist traditions and evaluate their analytical strengths and limitations.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Liberal feminism', '''Liberal feminism focuses on equal rights, legal personhood, access to education, employment and political participation. It often seeks reform through law, policy and institutional inclusion. Its strength is attention to formal barriers and equal opportunity. Critics argue that formal equality may leave deeper economic structures, unpaid care burdens and unequal power relations unchanged.

When evaluating a reform, ask whether the rule is discriminatory, whether implementation is effective and whether people have the resources and safety required to use the right.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Radical, Marxist and socialist feminism', '''Radical feminist approaches analyse patriarchy and gendered power, including violence, sexuality and control over bodies. Marxist feminism connects women’s oppression to class relations and the organisation of production. Socialist feminism examines the interaction of capitalism and patriarchy, including paid work and unpaid domestic labour. These traditions differ in how they explain the sources of inequality and which transformations they prioritise.

Do not collapse them into a single position. Compare their central explanatory mechanism, the institutions they identify and their preferred strategies for change.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Postcolonial and contemporary approaches', '''Postcolonial feminism challenges universal accounts that treat women in the Global South as one passive category and examines colonial histories, global power and local agency. Postmodern and poststructural approaches question fixed categories and examine how language, knowledge and institutions produce identities and hierarchies. Contemporary feminist scholarship also includes ecofeminist, queer, intersectional and transnational approaches.

A strong answer explains both the contribution and the limitation of a theory. Apply it to a specific issue and consider whose experiences are centred, whose are omitted and what evidence could challenge the argument.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Liberal feminism', 'Radical feminism', 'Marxist feminism', 'Socialist feminism', 'Postcolonial feminism', 'Poststructuralism', 'Gendered power'], examQuestion: 'Compare liberal and radical feminism in their explanations of gender inequality and proposed remedies.', practiceTask: 'Apply three feminist perspectives to the issue of women’s unpaid care work and compare the policy solutions each suggests.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '03', title: 'Gender, institutions, development and political participation', syllabusLink: 'Gender and family, education, economy, health, governance, political representation and development', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Gender across social and economic institutions', objectives: 'Assess how institutions influence gendered access to resources, services and decision-making.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Family, education and health', '''Families distribute care, time, resources and decision-making authority. Education can expand capabilities and economic options, but enrolment alone does not guarantee learning, safety, retention or transition into decent work. Health analysis includes access to services, nutrition, reproductive health, mental health, maternal care and the quality of treatment.

Reliable policy diagnosis uses disaggregated data and examines affordability, distance, safety, staff availability, social norms and service quality. Avoid explaining every disparity through culture alone when infrastructure, poverty or institutional design may also be decisive.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Work, economy and unpaid care', '''Gendered labour-market patterns include occupational segregation, wage differences, unequal access to assets and credit, informal work and disproportionate unpaid care responsibilities. Time-use data can reveal work that national accounts or employment statistics may not capture fully. Care infrastructure—such as childcare, transport, healthcare and social protection—can influence whether people can enter and remain in paid work.

A good economic analysis distinguishes participation, employment quality, earnings, control over income and long-term security. Policies should consider both formal-sector workers and people in informal or unpaid work.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Political participation and representation', '''Political participation includes voting, party membership, campaigning, public consultation, protest, leadership and elected office. Descriptive representation concerns whether representatives reflect the population; substantive representation concerns whether issues and interests are effectively advanced. Quotas can improve access to institutions but do not automatically guarantee influence, accountability or policy change.

Assess barriers such as campaign finance, party gatekeeping, violence and harassment, mobility, media treatment and care responsibilities. Evaluate representation through participation, committee influence, policy outcomes and accountability to constituents.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Gender mainstreaming', 'Capabilities', 'Labour-force participation', 'Unpaid care', 'Time-use survey', 'Descriptive representation', 'Substantive representation'], examQuestion: 'Discuss the relationship between unpaid care work and women’s economic participation.', practiceTask: 'Prepare a gender analysis of a public programme: identify beneficiaries, access barriers, resource control, decision-making and outcome indicators.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '04', title: 'Gender rights, policy and contemporary Pakistan', syllabusLink: 'International frameworks, constitutional and legal protections, violence, empowerment and gender policy in Pakistan', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Rights, institutions and gender-responsive policy', objectives: 'Connect rights frameworks to implementation challenges and practical reforms in Pakistan.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. International commitments and rights', '''The Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women (CEDAW) sets out state obligations to address discrimination in public and private life. The Sustainable Development Goals include commitments relevant to gender equality, education, health, decent work and participation. International commitments require domestic laws, institutions, budgets, data and enforcement to produce meaningful change.

When discussing a treaty, distinguish its legal obligations, reservations where relevant, domestic implementation and the practical capacity of institutions. Do not assume that ratification alone changes social outcomes.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Constitutional and legal context in Pakistan', '''Pakistan’s constitutional framework includes equality before law and protections relevant to non-discrimination and dignity. Statutory protections and institutions address areas such as workplace harassment, violence, family rights and representation, but laws and procedures differ across jurisdictions and may change. For current legal claims, consult the latest official legislation and judgments rather than relying on outdated summaries.

A CSS answer should distinguish constitutional principle, statute, institutional mandate, implementation and access to remedy. Discuss barriers such as reporting risks, legal awareness, costs, delays, evidence requirements and uneven service provision.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Policy design and evaluation', '''Gender-responsive policy begins with a defined problem and evidence about who is affected and why. Tools include gender budgeting, impact assessment, safe transport, accessible services, workplace protections, education retention programmes and support for survivors of violence. Effective design requires consultation, confidentiality, trained staff, referral pathways and monitoring.

Evaluation should measure outcomes rather than only activities. Count not just training sessions or laws passed, but changes in access, safety, income, learning, representation and quality of services. Build safeguards against backlash, tokenism and unintended exclusion.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['CEDAW', 'SDG 5', 'Gender budgeting', 'Gender mainstreaming', 'Equality before law', 'Workplace harassment', 'Gender-responsive policy'], examQuestion: 'Why does the existence of legal protections not always translate into gender equality? Discuss with reference to Pakistan.', practiceTask: 'Develop a one-page gender-responsive policy proposal with problem evidence, target group, legal basis, budget needs, delivery partners and outcome measures.'),
      ]),
    ],
  ),


  AcademyCourse(
    title: 'Sociology',
    marks: '100 marks • Group VII',
    description: 'A foundational course in sociological theory, research methods, culture, social institutions, stratification, social change and Pakistani society.',
    chapters: [
      AcademyChapter(number: '01', title: 'Sociology: concepts, perspectives and methods', syllabusLink: 'Nature and scope of sociology; sociological imagination; major theoretical perspectives; research methods', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Seeing society sociologically', objectives: 'Define sociology and use sociological perspectives to analyse patterns beyond individual explanations.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Sociology and sociological imagination', '''Sociology is the systematic study of social relationships, institutions, groups and patterns of behaviour. C. Wright Mills used the idea of the sociological imagination to connect personal troubles with public issues. An individual’s unemployment, for example, may be influenced by personal circumstances but also by labour-market structure, education systems, economic cycles and public policy. Sociology therefore asks how biographies are shaped by history and institutions.

The discipline examines both stability and change. It studies face-to-face interaction as well as large structures such as class, state, economy, education and religion. A sociological explanation should identify a social mechanism and support it with evidence rather than merely describing a social problem.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Major theoretical perspectives', '''Functionalism examines how institutions and social practices contribute to social order, while also raising questions about dysfunction and unequal consequences. Conflict perspectives focus on power, competition and the unequal distribution of resources. Symbolic interactionism studies how meaning is created through everyday interaction, symbols and interpretation. Feminist sociology analyses gendered power and the organisation of social life; postcolonial approaches examine how colonial histories and global hierarchies shape knowledge and institutions.

No single perspective explains every issue. Apply at least two perspectives to a topic and compare what each makes visible and what it may overlook.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Research methods and ethics', '''Quantitative methods use numerical data to identify distributions and relationships; qualitative methods examine meaning, experience and processes through interviews, observation and documents. Surveys can reach large samples but depend on question design and sampling. Interviews provide depth but require careful interpretation. Participant observation can reveal everyday practices while raising questions about access and researcher influence.

Research quality depends on clear concepts, valid measurement, reliable procedures, appropriate sampling and transparent analysis. Ethical research requires informed consent, confidentiality, minimising harm and honest reporting. Correlation alone does not establish causation; alternative explanations and selection effects must be considered.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Sociological imagination', 'Social structure', 'Functionalism', 'Conflict theory', 'Symbolic interactionism', 'Quantitative method', 'Qualitative method', 'Validity'], examQuestion: 'Explain the sociological imagination and show how it connects personal troubles with public issues.', practiceTask: 'Choose one social problem and analyse it through functionalist, conflict and interactionist perspectives. State what evidence would test each explanation.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '02', title: 'Culture, socialisation and social institutions', syllabusLink: 'Culture, norms, values, socialisation, family, education, religion, economy and political institutions', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'How societies reproduce and transform social life', objectives: 'Explain how culture and institutions shape behaviour, identity and opportunities.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Culture, norms and values', '''Culture includes shared meanings, knowledge, beliefs, practices, symbols and material objects. Values express broad ideas about what is desirable; norms are expectations governing conduct. Norms may be formal, such as laws, or informal, such as conventions. Sanctions reward conformity or discourage violations. Culture is not static or internally uniform: groups contest meanings and adapt practices under changing economic, technological and political conditions.

Ethnocentrism judges other cultures through the standards of one’s own group; cultural relativism seeks to understand practices in context. Contextual understanding does not require abandoning critical evaluation of harm or rights. A balanced sociological answer explains meaning, power and consequences.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Socialisation and institutions', '''Socialisation is the lifelong process through which people learn social expectations and develop identities. Primary socialisation often occurs in families; secondary socialisation occurs through schools, peers, workplaces, media and other institutions. Agents of socialisation can reinforce one another or communicate conflicting expectations.

Institutions organise recurring social needs and relationships. Family patterns vary by law, economy, kinship and culture. Education transmits knowledge but also sorts people into opportunities and may reproduce inequality. Religion can provide meaning, solidarity and moral frameworks while also being shaped by social and political contexts. Analyse institutions through both their stated functions and their unequal effects.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Deviance and social control', '''Deviance refers to behaviour or identities that violate social norms; it is not identical to crime, which is defined by law. Labelling perspectives examine how social reactions can shape identities and future treatment. Strain theories connect deviance to blocked opportunities and pressure; social-control approaches ask why people conform; conflict perspectives examine whose interests laws and enforcement serve.

Formal social control includes police, courts and administrative sanctions; informal control includes family, peers and community expectations. Examine the relationship between law, legitimacy, discrimination and due process. Avoid assuming that recorded crime rates directly measure all offending: reporting and enforcement practices affect the data.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Culture', 'Values', 'Norms', 'Socialisation', 'Social institution', 'Deviance', 'Labelling', 'Social control'], examQuestion: 'Discuss the role of family and education in socialisation and the reproduction of social inequality.', practiceTask: 'Map the agents of socialisation shaping one behaviour among young people. Distinguish formal rules, informal norms and institutional incentives.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '03', title: 'Stratification, social change and Pakistani society', syllabusLink: 'Class, status, gender, ethnicity, urbanisation, population, social movements and social change', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Inequality, mobility and social transformation', objectives: 'Analyse social stratification and evaluate processes of change in Pakistan.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Social stratification and mobility', '''Social stratification is the structured ranking of groups and individuals in society. Class analysis often considers ownership, occupation and market position; status concerns social honour or prestige; power concerns the ability to shape decisions and outcomes. These dimensions overlap but are not identical. Social mobility refers to movement within or between social positions and may be intergenerational or intragenerational.

Education, labour markets, inheritance, networks, discrimination and public policy influence mobility. Merit-based explanations may overlook unequal starting points and unequal access to quality institutions. A strong answer distinguishes individual achievement from structural opportunity.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Population, urbanisation and social change', '''Population change reflects fertility, mortality and migration. Demographic transition theory describes broad shifts from high birth and death rates toward lower rates, though timing and pathways differ across societies. Urbanisation involves more than city growth: it changes labour markets, housing, transport, family structures and political demands. Informal settlements may reflect gaps between migration, affordable housing and formal planning.

Social change can be driven by technology, education, economic restructuring, migration, conflict, social movements and state policy. Avoid presenting modernisation as a single linear path. Examine who benefits, who bears costs and how institutions adapt.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Pakistani society and social policy', '''Pakistan is socially diverse across language, region, class, gender, rural-urban location and livelihood. Social analysis should avoid treating the country as culturally uniform. Issues such as educational inequality, youth employment, urban services, rural livelihoods, family change and social protection are connected to institutional capacity and political economy.

Policy recommendations should be based on reliable and appropriately disaggregated evidence. Identify the population affected, the mechanism producing disadvantage, the responsible institution and indicators that can show whether conditions improve. Distinguish a national average from differences between provinces, districts and social groups.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Stratification', 'Class', 'Status', 'Social mobility', 'Demographic transition', 'Urbanisation', 'Social movement', 'Social policy'], examQuestion: 'Critically discuss the major forms of social stratification in Pakistan and their implications for social mobility.', practiceTask: 'Develop a short evidence-based note on one social issue in Pakistan, identifying structural causes, affected groups, institutional responsibility and feasible interventions.'),
      ]),
    ],
  ),
  AcademyCourse(
    title: 'Criminology',
    marks: '100 marks • Group VI',
    description: 'A structured introduction to crime, criminological theories, criminal justice institutions, policing, corrections, victimology and crime prevention.',
    chapters: [
      AcademyChapter(number: '01', title: 'Nature, scope and theories of crime', syllabusLink: 'Meaning of crime, criminology, schools of thought and classical, positivist and sociological explanations', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'What criminology studies', objectives: 'Define criminology and compare major explanations of criminal behaviour without reducing crime to one cause.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Crime and criminology', '''Crime is conduct prohibited by criminal law and subject to state-imposed penalties. Criminology is the systematic study of crime, offending, victimisation, social responses and crime prevention. The boundaries of crime vary across jurisdictions and change over time, so criminologists examine how laws are made and enforced as well as why individuals offend.

Official crime statistics are incomplete indicators. They are affected by reporting behaviour, police recording practices, legal definitions and enforcement priorities. Victimisation surveys, self-report studies, court data and qualitative research can provide additional perspectives. A sound analysis identifies what each source measures and what it leaves out.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Classical and positivist approaches', '''Classical criminology, associated with Cesare Beccaria and Jeremy Bentham, emphasises choice, deterrence and proportionate punishment. Deterrence is often discussed through certainty, swiftness and severity of sanctions; increasing severity alone may not produce the strongest preventive effect. Positivist approaches seek empirical explanations through biological, psychological and social factors, although early biological determinism has been heavily criticised for methodological and ethical reasons.

Modern criminology avoids treating offending as the product of one fixed trait. It examines interactions among individual circumstances, opportunity, peer networks, institutions and structural conditions. Distinguish explanation from excuse and empirical association from causal proof.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Sociological theories of crime', '''Strain theories examine pressure arising when socially valued goals are not matched by legitimate opportunities. Social learning approaches study how offending techniques and justifications may be learned through interaction. Social-control theories ask how bonds to family, school and community restrain offending. Labelling approaches examine how official reactions and stigma can shape later opportunities and identity. Routine activity theory focuses on the convergence of a motivated offender, suitable target and absence of capable guardianship.

Apply theories to specific forms of crime and identify their limits. A theory that helps explain street theft may not explain corporate fraud or cybercrime equally well.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Criminology', 'Deterrence', 'Classical school', 'Positivism', 'Strain theory', 'Social learning', 'Social control', 'Routine activity'], examQuestion: 'Compare classical and positivist approaches to crime and assess the relevance of sociological theories.', practiceTask: 'Apply three criminological theories to one offence type. Identify the mechanism each proposes and evidence that could support or challenge it.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '02', title: 'Criminal justice institutions and procedure', syllabusLink: 'Police, investigation, prosecution, courts, due process, prisons and criminal justice administration', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'From investigation to adjudication', objectives: 'Explain the roles of criminal justice institutions and the importance of due process and institutional coordination.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Police and investigation', '''Police organisations prevent and detect crime, respond to incidents, investigate allegations and maintain public order under law. Effective investigation depends on lawful powers, evidence preservation, trained personnel, forensic capability, documentation and supervision. Community trust influences whether witnesses report offences and cooperate with investigators.

Police performance should not be judged only by arrest numbers or reported crime totals. Relevant measures include response times, case quality, lawful conduct, public confidence, complaint resolution and the proportion of investigations that produce reliable evidence. Accountability mechanisms and independent complaint review help guard against abuse.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Prosecution and courts', '''Prosecutors assess evidence, make charging decisions under applicable law and present cases fairly. Courts determine guilt according to the applicable standard of proof and protect procedural rights. Coordination among police, prosecutors, forensic services and courts can reduce avoidable delays, but institutional independence must be preserved.

Due process includes lawful arrest, access to counsel, fair hearing, impartial adjudication and protection against arbitrary punishment. Presumption of innocence is a central safeguard. A strong answer distinguishes an allegation, an arrest, a charge and a conviction.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Prisons and corrections', '''Correctional institutions have functions that include secure custody, lawful punishment, rehabilitation and preparation for reintegration. Prison overcrowding, limited healthcare, weak education and vocational opportunities, violence and poor case management can undermine these objectives. Community-based sanctions and probation may be appropriate for some offenders, depending on law, risk and public safety.

Rehabilitation requires individual assessment, evidence-informed programmes, continuity of support and links to education, employment and family services. Evaluate correctional outcomes through reoffending, safety, health, reintegration and respect for legal rights.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Investigation', 'Evidence preservation', 'Prosecution', 'Due process', 'Presumption of innocence', 'Adjudication', 'Probation', 'Rehabilitation'], examQuestion: 'Explain the role of police, prosecution and courts in ensuring an effective and rights-respecting criminal justice system.', practiceTask: 'Map a hypothetical case from complaint to judgment. Identify evidence, institutional responsibilities, due-process safeguards and likely coordination failures.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '03', title: 'Victimology, crime prevention and contemporary crime', syllabusLink: 'Victimology, juvenile justice, gender-based violence, organised crime, corruption, cybercrime and prevention strategies', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Victims, prevention and emerging challenges', objectives: 'Assess victim-centred responses and design prevention strategies suited to different crime problems.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Victimology and victim support', '''Victimology studies victimisation, its causes and consequences, and the response of institutions and society. Victims may experience physical injury, psychological harm, financial loss, stigma and repeated trauma through insensitive procedures. A victim-centred system provides safety, information, privacy, referral services and meaningful participation without compromising fair-trial rights.

Victim-blaming assumes that a victim’s behaviour caused or justified an offence. Criminological analysis instead examines offender conduct, opportunity structures and institutional response. Data collection must protect confidentiality and avoid exposing vulnerable people to retaliation.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Crime prevention and juvenile justice', '''Primary prevention seeks to reduce risk before offending occurs through education, safer environments and social support. Secondary prevention targets groups or places with elevated risk; tertiary prevention seeks to prevent repeat offending. Situational prevention changes opportunity through guardianship, design and security, while social prevention addresses underlying risks and protective factors.

Children in conflict with the law require age-appropriate procedures, safeguards and rehabilitation consistent with applicable law and rights standards. Diversion may reduce harmful exposure to formal justice systems where legally available and suitable. Evaluate programmes through outcomes and unintended effects, not only activity counts.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Organised, cyber and economic crime', '''Organised crime can involve structured cooperation for sustained illegal profit, including trafficking, illicit markets and financial crime. Cybercrime uses digital systems as targets or instruments and may cross borders, complicating evidence collection and jurisdiction. Economic crime includes fraud, corruption, money laundering and abuse of entrusted power. These offences require financial investigation, digital forensics, inter-agency cooperation and safeguards for privacy and due process.

Prevention combines regulation, transparent procurement, secure systems, whistle-blower protection, public awareness and capable investigation. In Pakistan-related answers, distinguish federal and provincial mandates and verify current laws and institutional responsibilities from official sources.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Victimology', 'Victim-centred approach', 'Primary prevention', 'Situational prevention', 'Juvenile justice', 'Organised crime', 'Cybercrime', 'Financial investigation'], examQuestion: 'Discuss the role of victim-centred justice and evidence-based crime prevention in improving public confidence.', practiceTask: 'Design a prevention plan for online financial fraud: identify targets, offender methods, guardianship gaps, reporting channels and evaluation indicators.'),
      ]),
    ],
  ),


  AcademyCourse(
    title: 'Environmental Sciences',
    marks: '100 marks • Group V',
    description: 'A foundations course in ecosystems, biodiversity, pollution, climate change, environmental governance, impact assessment and sustainable development.',
    chapters: [
      AcademyChapter(number: '01', title: 'Ecological foundations and natural resources', syllabusLink: 'Ecology, ecosystems, energy flow, biogeochemical cycles, biodiversity and resource management', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Ecosystems, biodiversity and resource use', objectives: 'Explain ecosystem structure and assess the relationship between biodiversity and human well-being.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Ecosystem structure and function', '''An ecosystem consists of living organisms and the physical environment interacting through energy flows and nutrient cycles. Producers convert energy, commonly through photosynthesis, into organic matter; consumers obtain energy by feeding; decomposers break down organic material and return nutrients to the system. Food chains and food webs show feeding relationships, while trophic levels describe positions in energy transfer.

Energy transfer between trophic levels is inefficient, so available energy generally declines at higher levels. Carbon, nitrogen and water cycles move materials through atmosphere, water, soil and living organisms. Disturbance can alter ecosystem processes, but resilience depends on diversity, connectivity, scale and the intensity of pressure.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Biodiversity and conservation', '''Biodiversity includes genetic diversity, species diversity and ecosystem diversity. It supports food systems, pollination, soil fertility, water regulation, climate resilience and cultural values. Threats include habitat loss and fragmentation, overexploitation, invasive species, pollution and climate change. Conservation may be in situ, protecting species in natural habitats, or ex situ, using facilities such as seed banks and botanical collections.

Protected areas are important but insufficient without enforcement, local participation, sustainable livelihoods and landscape connectivity. Evaluate conservation through ecological outcomes and social legitimacy. Communities should not bear disproportionate costs for benefits enjoyed elsewhere.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Natural resources and sustainability', '''Renewable resources can regenerate if use remains within ecological limits; non-renewable resources are finite on human timescales. Water, forests, soils, minerals and energy require different management approaches. Common-pool resources can be depleted when access rules and collective governance are weak. Elinor Ostrom’s work shows that communities can develop effective institutions for managing shared resources under appropriate conditions.

Sustainable development integrates environmental protection, economic opportunity and social equity. It requires attention to intergenerational effects, distribution of costs and benefits, and the limits of natural systems. Avoid treating sustainability as a slogan: identify the resource, pressure, governance mechanism and measurable outcome.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Ecosystem', 'Trophic level', 'Biogeochemical cycle', 'Biodiversity', 'In situ conservation', 'Ex situ conservation', 'Common-pool resource', 'Sustainability'], examQuestion: 'Explain the importance of biodiversity and critically assess the major pressures threatening it in Pakistan.', practiceTask: 'Prepare a conservation case study identifying ecosystem services, threats, stakeholders, governance options and indicators of ecological recovery.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '02', title: 'Pollution, climate change and environmental health', syllabusLink: 'Air, water and soil pollution; waste management; climate change; environmental health and disaster risk', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Environmental pressures and their consequences', objectives: 'Explain pollution pathways and climate risks and distinguish mitigation from adaptation.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Air, water and soil pollution', '''Air pollution includes particulate matter and harmful gases from transport, industry, power generation, waste burning and household fuels. Health impacts depend on pollutant concentration, exposure duration and vulnerability. Water pollution can arise from untreated sewage, industrial discharge, agricultural runoff and unsafe waste disposal. Soil contamination can affect food chains, groundwater and ecosystem function.

Pollution control follows a hierarchy: prevent generation where possible, reduce emissions, reuse and recycle materials, treat residual waste and safely dispose of what remains. Monitoring requires reliable sampling, transparent standards and enforcement. A policy should address both pollution sources and exposure of affected communities.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Climate science and impacts', '''The enhanced greenhouse effect occurs when human activities increase atmospheric concentrations of heat-trapping gases, changing Earth’s energy balance. Climate change affects temperature, precipitation patterns, glaciers, sea levels and the frequency or intensity of some extremes. Risk depends on hazard, exposure and vulnerability; the same hazard can produce very different impacts in different communities.

Pakistan is exposed to multiple climate risks, including heat stress, floods, droughts, glacial hazards and coastal impacts. Climate vulnerability is shaped by settlement patterns, infrastructure, poverty, land management, early warning and institutional capacity. Distinguish weather events from long-term climate trends and use attribution evidence carefully.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Mitigation, adaptation and disaster risk reduction', '''Mitigation reduces greenhouse-gas emissions or increases removals through cleaner energy, efficiency, transport, land management and industrial changes. Adaptation reduces harm by adjusting systems to actual or expected climate impacts, for example through resilient infrastructure, water management, heat plans and climate-informed agriculture. Disaster risk reduction addresses prevention, preparedness, response and recovery.

Effective climate policy combines national planning with local knowledge and financing. Evaluate interventions through avoided losses, emissions reductions, distributional effects and resilience outcomes. Avoid presenting mitigation and adaptation as substitutes; both are necessary for different dimensions of risk.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Particulate matter', 'Eutrophication', 'Greenhouse effect', 'Climate vulnerability', 'Mitigation', 'Adaptation', 'Disaster risk reduction', 'Resilience'], examQuestion: 'Differentiate climate-change mitigation and adaptation and discuss why Pakistan requires both approaches.', practiceTask: 'Design a district-level climate risk profile: identify hazards, exposed groups, vulnerabilities, early-warning gaps and adaptation priorities.'),
      ]),
      AcademyChapter(number: '03', title: 'Environmental governance and sustainable development', syllabusLink: 'Environmental law and institutions, EIA, environmental economics, international agreements and sustainable development', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Institutions, assessment and environmental policy', objectives: 'Explain how environmental decisions are governed and evaluate the role of assessment and economic instruments.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Environmental governance and law', '''Environmental governance includes laws, institutions, standards, public participation, monitoring and enforcement. Responsibilities may be distributed across levels of government and across sectors such as energy, agriculture, transport and industry. Fragmented mandates can create gaps, while coordination and transparent information can improve implementation.

The polluter-pays principle assigns pollution costs to those responsible; the precautionary principle supports preventive action where serious harm is plausible despite scientific uncertainty; the prevention principle prioritises avoiding damage at source. These principles guide policy but require workable institutions, evidence and fair procedures.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Environmental impact assessment', '''Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) examines likely environmental and social effects of a proposed project before major decisions are finalised. Typical stages include screening, scoping, baseline study, impact prediction, alternatives analysis, mitigation planning, public consultation, review and monitoring. Strategic Environmental Assessment applies similar reasoning to policies, plans and programmes at an earlier and broader level.

An EIA is useful only when it can influence project design and when commitments are monitored. Weak baseline data, limited consultation, conflicts of interest and poor enforcement can turn assessment into paperwork. Evaluate the quality of the process, not merely whether a report exists.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Environmental economics and global cooperation', '''Environmental externalities occur when private decisions impose costs or benefits on others not reflected in market prices. Instruments include pollution taxes, tradable permits, standards, subsidies and payments for ecosystem services. Each has distributional and administrative implications. Cost-benefit analysis can inform choices but must handle uncertainty, long time horizons, non-market values and equity transparently.

Environmental problems often cross borders. The Paris Agreement provides a framework for nationally determined climate commitments and international cooperation. Other multilateral agreements address biodiversity, wetlands, hazardous substances and ozone-depleting substances. Assess agreements through commitments, finance, technology, monitoring and implementation—not declarations alone.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Environmental governance', 'Polluter pays', 'Precautionary principle', 'EIA', 'Strategic assessment', 'Externality', 'Carbon pricing', 'Paris Agreement'], examQuestion: 'Critically evaluate the effectiveness of environmental impact assessment as a tool for sustainable development.', practiceTask: 'Review a hypothetical infrastructure project and outline its EIA scope, alternatives, consultation plan, mitigation hierarchy and monitoring indicators.'),
      ]),
    ],
  ),
  AcademyCourse(
    title: 'Business Administration',
    marks: '100 marks • Group III',
    description: 'A structured introduction to management, organisational behaviour, human resources, marketing, operations, finance and strategic decision-making.',
    chapters: [
      AcademyChapter(number: '01', title: 'Management foundations and organisational theory', syllabusLink: 'Management functions, schools of thought, organisational structure, leadership and decision-making', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Management as coordinated organisational work', objectives: 'Explain management functions and compare organisational structures and leadership approaches.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Management functions', '''Management coordinates people and resources to achieve organisational objectives. Common functions include planning, organising, leading and controlling. Planning defines goals and routes to achieve them; organising allocates responsibilities and resources; leading guides and motivates people; controlling compares performance with standards and supports corrective action. These functions overlap and recur rather than operating as a rigid sequence.

Effectiveness concerns whether the right objectives are achieved; efficiency concerns the relationship between outputs and resources used. Organisations need both. A low-cost process that fails customer or public requirements is not effective, while an effective outcome achieved with avoidable waste may not be sustainable.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Organisational structure and design', '''Structure defines reporting relationships, roles, authority and coordination. Functional structures group work by specialisation; divisional structures organise around products, regions or customers; matrix structures combine dimensions and can improve cross-functional coordination while creating dual-reporting tensions. Centralisation concentrates decision authority; decentralisation distributes it closer to operations.

The appropriate design depends on strategy, size, environment, technology and workforce capabilities. Formal structure should be distinguished from informal networks through which information and influence also move. Analyse coordination costs, accountability and adaptability when recommending a design.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Leadership and decision-making', '''Leadership approaches include directive, participative, transactional, transformational and situational styles. Their usefulness depends on task uncertainty, staff competence, urgency and organisational culture. Decision-making may be programmed for recurring issues or non-programmed for novel problems. Managers should define the problem, gather relevant evidence, generate alternatives, assess risks, decide, implement and review outcomes.

Group decisions can improve information but may also produce conformity or diffusion of responsibility. Encourage constructive dissent, clarify decision rights and record assumptions. Evaluate a decision by process quality and outcomes under the information available at the time.'''),
